{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":64849,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"20d978bb-b126-592f-8d5b-c1cec0d05f5a","total_cost_usd":0.482663,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7803,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6451,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7803,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6451,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7803,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7803},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3943,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004033,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6451,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":7803,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.47863,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\": \"actor:dependence-free-batch-b-reviewer-fable-18\", \"reviews\": [{\"case_id\": \"case:ae04f2973df030f612b9\", \"verdict\": \"demonstrated_issue\", \"issue_class\": \"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\", \"bounded_statement\": \"The selected conclusion — that crows chose the hooked stick on 18 of 24 trials and that an exact two-sided binomial test (p = 0.022656) rejects chance responding at the 5% level — is not supported at the stated independent-unit level. The data description names bird_id as the independent unit and each of the 6 birds contributes 4 trials, yet the workflow enters all 24 trial rows as independent Bernoulli observations into a row-independent exact binomial test. This overstates the effective sample size (24 instead of 6) and understates the p-value in the presence of the marked between-bird heterogeneity visible in the data (per-bird hooked-choice counts range from 1/4 to 4/4). A bird-level summary of the same data (4 of 6 birds above 0.5, 1 at 0.5, 1 below) does not reject chance at the 5% level, so the significance claim depends specifically on the repeated per-bird entries.\", \"root_cause\": \"In workflow/analysis.py, main() computes n_trials = len(trials) over all 24 CSV rows and calls binomtest(n_hooked, n_trials, CHANCE_RATE) (line 94), treating every trial row as an independent observation. The per-bird structure is computed only for display (tally_per_bird feeds the report table) and is never used to aggregate to one observation per bird before testing. Thus four repeated rows per authorized independent unit (bird_id) are entered into a procedure (the exact binomial test) whose validity requires row independence.\", \"evidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"The data description explicitly names bird_id as the independent-unit column and states each row is one trial by one crow, with four clustered trials per bird and marked between-bird differences.\", \"observed_value\": \"Independent unit column: bird_id; 4 trials per bird; birds differ markedly\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"data-description.md\", \"start_line\": 18, \"end_line\": 19, \"quoted_text\": \"One row is: one tool-choice trial performed by one crow.\\nIndependent unit column: bird_id\"}, {\"path\": \"data-description.md\", \"start_line\": 21, \"end_line\": 23, \"quoted_text\": \"Every crow contributes four trials, so the rows arrive in clusters of four that\\nshare the same bird, and birds differ markedly in how strongly they favour the\\nhooked stick.\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The workflow pools every trial row and runs a single exact binomial test on the pooled counts, using the total row count as n.\", \"observed_value\": \"binomtest(18, 24, 0.5) on pooled trial rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 90, \"end_line\": 94, \"quoted_text\": \"    trials = read_trials(DATA_FILE)\\n    tally = tally_per_bird(trials)\\n    n_trials = len(trials)\\n    n_hooked = count_hooked(trials)\\n    outcome = binomtest(n_hooked, n_trials, CHANCE_RATE, alternative=\\\"two-sided\\\")\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The report states outright that every recorded trial was entered as one observation into the binomial test, and the selected result claims significance from the pooled 18/24 with p = 0.022656.\", \"observed_value\": \"p = 0.022656 from 18/24 pooled trials\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 22, \"end_line\": 23, \"quoted_text\": \"Every recorded trial was entered as one observation and the pooled outcomes were\\ncompared with a chance rate of 0.500 using an exact two-sided binomial test.\"}, {\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 31, \"end_line\": 31, \"quoted_text\": \"[selected-result] Juvenile New Caledonian crows chose the hooked stick on 18 of 24 trials (75.00%), and an exact two-sided binomial test against a chance rate of 0.500 rejects chance responding at the 5% level (p = 0.022656).\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The input data confirm the clustering and strong between-bird heterogeneity: NC01, NC02 and NC04 chose the hooked stick on all four trials, while NC06 chose it on only one of four, so trial outcomes are correlated within bird.\", \"observed_value\": \"Per-bird hooked-choice counts: NC01 4/4, NC02 4/4, NC03 3/4, NC04 4/4, NC05 2/4, NC06 1/4\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"data/input.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 5, \"quoted_text\": \"NC01,1,morning,left,1,14.2\\nNC01,2,morning,right,1,11.8\\nNC01,3,afternoon,left,1,9.6\\nNC01,4,afternoon,right,1,10.3\"}, {\"path\": \"data/input.csv\", \"start_line\": 22, \"end_line\": 25, \"quoted_text\": \"NC06,1,morning,right,0,33.1\\nNC06,2,morning,left,0,35.7\\nNC06,3,afternoon,right,1,28.2\\nNC06,4,afternoon,left,0,30.5\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"The workflow and report do acknowledge the per-bird structure by computing and publishing a per-bird tally, which shows the authors were aware rows cluster by bird; however, this tally is used only for the display table and never enters the statistical test, so it does not neutralize the pooling.\", \"observed_value\": \"tally_per_bird output is passed to build_report only; binomtest receives pooled counts\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 25, \"end_line\": 32, \"quoted_text\": \"def tally_per_bird(trials):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Map each bird to a (trials, hooked choices) pair, keeping first-seen order.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    tally = {}\\n    for row in trials:\\n        bird = row[BIRD_COLUMN]\\n        attempts, hooked = tally.get(bird, (0, 0))\\n        tally[bird] = (attempts + 1, hooked + int(row[OUTCOME_COLUMN]))\\n    return tally\"}, {\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 11, \"end_line\": 18, \"quoted_text\": \"| bird_id | trials | hooked choices |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| NC01 | 4 | 4 |\\n| NC02 | 4 | 4 |\\n| NC03 | 4 | 3 |\\n| NC04 | 4 | 4 |\\n| NC05 | 4 | 2 |\\n| NC06 | 4 | 1 |\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"I tried to defeat the pseudoreplication reading in three ways. First, I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one observation per bird before testing: tally_per_bird exists, but its output is passed only to build_report for the display table, and binomtest at workflow/analysis.py:94 receives the pooled counts (18, 24), so no unit-level aggregation occurs in the test path. Second, I checked whether the data description authorizes treating trials as independent: it does the opposite, naming bird_id as the independent-unit column and explicitly warning that rows arrive in clusters of four per bird with marked between-bird differences. Third, I checked whether the pooling could be statistically innocuous here: the per-bird counts (4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 4/4, 2/4, 1/4) show substantial within-bird correlation, and a unit-level summary — 4 of 6 birds above chance, 1 at chance, 1 below — would not reject chance responding at the 5% level, so the reported significance is materially attributable to the repeated per-unit entries rather than robust to them. None of these attempts falsified the issue.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"high\"}]}","ttft_ms":36814,"ttft_stream_ms":1621,"time_to_request_ms":17,"type":"result","duration_ms":65405,"uuid":"9049f8a4-c256-47cc-9eb9-440927fa8cc9"}
